\f0\fs68 \cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Better Harness: A Recipe for Harness Hill-Climbing with Evals\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 TL;DR:
\f0\b0  We can build better agents by building better harnesses. But to autonomously build a \'93better\'94 harness, we need a strong learning signal to \'93hill-climb\'94 on. We share how we use evals as that signal, plus design decisions that help our agent 
\f1\b generalize
\f0\b0  instead of 
\f1\b overfit.  
\f0\b0 Better-Harness is a prototype system for iteratively sourcing and improving your harness with evals.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Evals are training data for Agents\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 In classical machine learning, training data guides the model\'92s learning process. Each training example contributes a gradient that updates the model\'92s weights toward \'93correctness.\'94 We have a similar learning loop for agents.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 model + training data + gradient descent \uc0\u8594  better model
\f0\b0 \cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 harness + evals + harness engineering \uc0\u8594  better agent
\f0\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/Vtrivedy10/article/2041927488918413589/media/2041729716890046464"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs30 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\
}}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 Evals encode the behavior we want our agent to exhibit in production.
\f0\b0  They\'92re the "training data" for harness engineering. Each eval case contributes a signal like \'93did the agent take the right action\'94 or \'93produce the right outcome?\'94 That signal guides the next proposed edit to the harness.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 The same rigor and care we put into data quality and curation for model training should also go into eval design. We discuss the importance of data quality in a previous post, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.langchain.com/how-we-build-evals-for-deep-agents/"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 \strokec2 how we build evals for Deep Agents}}.\cb1 \
\cb3 There\'92s some great recent work that formalize the steps to optimize harnesses including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.28052"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 \strokec2 Meta-Harness}} from Stanford and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.03329"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 \strokec2 Auto-Harness}} from DeepMind. We also previously shared a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.langchain.com/improving-deep-agents-with-harness-engineering/"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 \strokec2 Harness Improvement Loop to hill-climb Terminal Bench 2.0}} by just tweaking the harness layer.  We think there\'92s great future work to be done around the update algorithm itself, but harness improvement is a compound system that goes beyond the update algorithm which is what we talk about here.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 Better-Harness is a take on compound systems engineering.
\f0\b0 \cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 data sourcing \uc0\u8594  experiment design \'97> optimization \'97> review & acceptance
\f0\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 So we include practical details that go alongside the update loop such as how we source evals in the first place, how we design against overfitting, store Traces over time, and manually review updates to sanity check anything we ship to production.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Sourcing good evals\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 Evals are the foundation that power the harness hill-climbing process. Here are the practical ways we source, curate, and use them.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 Hand-curated.
\f0\b0  For any given task, the team manually writes examples that capture what we think the agent should do in production.  These are often high value, but difficult to generate at scale.\cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 Production traces.
\f0\b0  Every agent interaction generates a trace where failures become eval cases.  Mining traces for eval material is the leverage, high-throughput way to improve evals over time. Even before running an agent over evals, often a team dogfooding our agent will report errors directly in Slack with a Trace link.  We recommend dogfooding agents and directly sharing feedback for everyone to see, it helps build shared knowledge of agent behavior.\cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 External datasets.
\f0\b0  These datasets are useful but need to be manually curated to make sure the test cases used to improve the agent reflect desired behaviors. Often each task is adjusted to make sure they measure the important behavior.\cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 Tag everything.
\f0\b0  Every eval gets tagged to behavioral categories: "tool selection," "multi-step reasoning," etc. Tags enable meaningful holdout sets and targeted experiments.  It also saves a lot of money because we can run subsets of evals.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Building learning systems that generalize\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 The ideal outcome for any learning system is 
\f1\b generalization
\f0\b0 . We give an input signal that captures the distribution of behaviors we want in the wild. The system fits to it and then \'93just works\'94 on new inputs it's never seen.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 The obvious problem:
\f0\b0  We don't have unlimited data.\cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 The fix:
\f0\b0  Encode important behaviors into curated evals. Quality > quantity, a small set of well-tagged evals covering the behaviors you care about beats thousands of noisy but high-coverage evals.\cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 The subtle problem \uc0\u8594  agents are famous cheaters:
\f0\b0  Any learning system is prone to reward hacking where the agent overfits its structure to make the existing evals pass that it can see. This makes sense because the loop just wants to \'93make number go up\'94 and doesn't know about generalization. We prompt to avoid overfitting but it isn\'92t perfect.\cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 The fix:
\f0\b0  Holdout sets become a proxy for true generalization. We\'92ve seen approaches that  We pair with human review as a second signal and we get semi-automated systems can improve scores while avoiding behaviors we don\'92t want in prod.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Better-Harness: a recipe for hill climbing your harness\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 We created a scaffold for autonomously improving our harness using evals as a signal in each step.  A research version is open sourced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents/tree/main/examples/better-harness"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 \strokec2 here}}, here are the main steps:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Source and tag evals.
\f0\b0  This is a mix of hand-writing evals, mining them from production traces, and using/adapting external datasets. We tag each eval to behavioral categories (like multi-step retrieval) and regularly remove evals that are saturated or we longer feel are useful for the agent + current generation of models.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Split data per category.
\f0\b0  Create Optimization and Holdout sets.  This is very important!  We find that autonomouos hill-climbing has a tendency to overfit to tasks so holdout sets ensure that learned optimizations work on previously unseen data, though the general distirbution should match existing evals.  This mirrors what production will look like.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Run a Baseline.
\f0\b0  Run a baseline experiment on the Optimization & Holdout sets before any edits.  This grounds all updates in the update steps.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Optimize.
\f0\b0  Each iteration runs autonomously with optional human review.  
\f1\b Diagnose
\f0\b0  errors from traces.  
\f1\b Experiment 
\f0\b0 with a targeted harness change. We scope to one change at a time to avoid confounding but that may mean updating a prompt and tool simultaneously so the system works well together.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Validate:
\f0\b0  In each step, the loop checks to make sure that the proposed change helped pass new evals while avoiding regressions on existing passing cases.  It\'92s common that some change results in a net overall score gain with some regressions.  The agent gets context of these regressions so it can try to fix them in the next update without losing the gains from the existing update.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Human review.
\f0\b0  We manually review changes and edge cases metrics miss.  This often includes instructions that are overfit to the optimization set and although they don\'92t hurt generalization, they end up being a waste of tokens. This gives us another sanity check and gate against overfitting.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/Vtrivedy10/article/2041927488918413589/media/2041730050656002048"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs30 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\
}}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Examples of harness changes\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 Here are the kinds of changes the optimization loop can discover and validate:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 Prompt and instruction updates.
\f0\b0  The most common change. The agent keeps misinterpreting a tool's output format, or it's too aggressive about calling a tool when it should ask a clarifying question first. The fix is a targeted instruction update addition like "when querying multiple files that have dependent information, offload information to the filesystem and re-aggregate before giving a final answer."\cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 Adding or updating a tool or tool description.
\f0\b0  The agent may fail contextualizing when to use a new tool.  Edits include examples on of how to use, how to chain this tool, an updated tool description, and editing the overall tool suite to disambiguate similar tools\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Results from the Better-Harness loop\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 We tested this approach with Claude Sonnet 4.6 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://z.ai/"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 \strokec2 Z.ai}}\'92s GLM-5 on a subset of our evals.  
\f1\b Note:
\f0\b0  We have other work underway generalizing Better-Harness across many models in deepagents using a bigger eval suite.  The goal is to publish a series of 
\f1\b model profiles
\f0\b0  that capture the nuances of each model tuned for our evals as a public artifact.\cb1 \
\cb3 We assembled a small representative sample from existing eval categories and split that sample into a set for hill-climbing and holdout to evaluate generalization. With large or expensive eval sets, we suggest representative/stratified sampling to give a good set to hill-climb against.  Once this works well, it can be scaled up to the larger set.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 Main experiment goal:
\f0\b0  discover & fix failure modes over our evals.  Port general changes that increase eval performance back to the harness.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 We previously observed failure modes such as over-asking follow-up questions and errors in chaining together new tools. After hill climbing on the optimization set, we evaluated the final harness on the holdout using two categories,
\f1\b  tool_selection
\f0\b0  and 
\f1\b followup_quality
\f0\b0 .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/Vtrivedy10/article/2041927488918413589/media/2041730453917282304"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs30 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\
}}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 The results were strong on both models and both. Both get nearly fully generalization to the holdout set which covered the same capability with totally unseen examples.\cb1 \
\cb3 Many gains are from more explicit instructions around discovered failure modes.  Here are a few concrete examples the optimization loop discovered that we found interesting.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/Vtrivedy10/article/2041927488918413589/media/2041730731383406592"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs30 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\
}}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 For evals that inject new tools into the default harness like search-then-email , the loop discovered better descriptions of how to use and compose those tools.  This is promising for builders creating vertical agents across domains, because optimization loops adapt well to the task specifics in context.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Evals maintenance & regressions\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 Along with hill climbing, evals also explicitly capture and protect against regressions over time.  Once our agent handles a case correctly, we don\'92t want to lose that gain. The eval becomes a regression test. This is similar to ideas in traditional software engineering like Test Driven Development (TDD). Some regressions are bound to happen across many changes over time so we select a subset of evals that we always want to pass and look at our run suspiciously if these suddenly fail.\cb1 \
\cb3 We don\'92t think our eval suite should grow monotonically, spring cleaning of evals is good! We regularly assess whether an eval is still useful because of more intelligent models or a different behavior we want for the agent.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 The Future: automated error detection & fixes\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 This approach works because 
\f1\b traces give us a dense feedback signal
\f0\b0 . Evals benefit from traces to compare across versions and numerically ground which changes contribute to a better score (which should be a good proxy for a better user experience).\cb1 \
\cb3 Overall, we point agentic compute at traces to:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Derive errors automatically.
\f0\b0  We want to constantly monitor our agent traces to classify and cluster failures in production.\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Generate evals from production.
\f0\b0  A trace where the agent made a mistake is an eval case. A trace where a user corrected the agent is even better. The flywheel: more usage \uc0\u8594  more traces \u8594  more evals \u8594  better harness\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Compare harness versions.
\f0\b0  Side-by-side trace comparisons show what changed in the harness that contributed to new behavior\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 Every trace contains valuable data to produce a potential eval. And every (good) eval makes the harness better.  To facilitate this, all agent runs are logged to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://smith.langchain.com/"}}{\fldrslt 
\f1\b \cf2 \ul \ulc2 \strokec2 LangSmith}} with full traces. This gives us trace-level diagnosis for the optimization loop, production monitoring for regression detection, and trace mining for eval generation.\cb1 \
\cb3 Our main takeaways and ongoing work:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 Evals are training data for autonomous harness engineering.
\f0\b0  The same principles that make ML training work such as data quality, train/test splits, and generalization checks apply to agent development.\cb1 \

\f1\b \cb3 Fitting models to harnesses.
\f0\b0   There\'92s a large amount of work that goes into fitting every model to its harness.  For example, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://developers.openai.com/cookbook/examples/gpt-5/codex_prompting_guide"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 \strokec2 codex prompting guide}} suggests a certain format for their Edit tool.  This requires a bigger search search space and eval set, we\'92re excited to share real examples of what that looks like for any team looking to do this.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 Overall, tracing and maintaining good evals is what makes this system work in practice. Invest in this early with your team and come build the future of autonomously improving agents.  We open sourced a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagents/tree/main/examples/better-harness"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc2 \strokec2 research version of this scaffold}} for builders to experiment with.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
